Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Amend Addenda  Type  Surgical Pathology  Specimen Type  Breast  excisional tissue  Procedure Date  REMOVED_DATE  Ordering Provider  BETH ANN LESNIKOSKI M D   CASE  BS 07  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME              Addended Report              Resident  Shakti Ramkissoon  M D   Ph D   Justine A Barletta  M D   Pathologist  Deborah A Dillon  M D   PATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS   RIGHT BREAST  WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A CLIP   CARCINOMA IN SITU with mixed ductal and lobular features  solid type   intermediate nuclear grade   with necrosis  is present in three blocks   focally in association with biopsy site changes  see note    CARCINOMA IN SITU  solid type  low nuclear grade   is extensively present  see  note    Three foci of MICROINVASION are present  largest focus measuring 0 1cm  slides   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    Microinvasion is confirmed on immunohistochemistry for  p63  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    No lymphovascular invasion is identified   Microinvasive carcinoma is present  0 1cm from the deep margin  slides 10 and  20   All other margins are negative   0 3cm   for invasive carcinoma   CARCINOMA IN SITU  intermediate nuclear grade  is present 0 2cm from the deep  margin  slide 14    CARCINOMA IN SITU  low nuclear grade  is present at or  0 1cm from all margins   see note    ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA is present  slide 19    Calcifications present in association with benign epithelium   NOTE  The low grade in situ component is present in twenty three of twenty four  blocks of tissue  including at or  0 1cm from superior  inferior  anterior   posterior  medial and lateral margins  Although this low grade in situ  carcinoma morphologically resembles LCIS  its association with intermediate  grade CIS with necrosis  the association of both low grade and  intermediate grade CIS with multifocal microinvasion and the absence of  immunoreactivity for progesterone receptor in both the low grade and  intermediate grade CIS are unusual and suggest that both the low grade and the  intermediate grade CIS may not behave as usual lobular in situ neoplasia   Slides also seen by Dr  Susan Lester  who concurs   CLINICAL DATA   History  53 year old female with R breast cancer in situ carcinoma with mixed  ductal and lobular features   Operation  R breast wire localized partial mastectomy   Operative Findings  Not provided   Clinical Diagnosis  R breast DCIS   TISSUE SUBMITTED   A 1  R breast tissue with wire  standard in please don t apply extra ink to  crevis sic  on anterior margin    RADIOLOGY   Specimen has wire and clip  Dr  BAL called SR   GROSS DESCRIPTION   The specimen is received fresh  labeled with the patient s name  and unit  number  and   1  Right breast tissue with wire   and consists of a REMOVED_DATE  medial  to lateral  x REMOVED_DATE  inferior to superior  x REMOVED_DATE  anterior to posterior cm  breast biopsy with a localization wire in place  which enters the specimen at  the lateral anterior border  An accompanying specimen radiograph demonstrates  a clip along the superior medial margins  The specimen is inked per pathology  protocol for the evaluation of margins  posterior black  anterior blue   superior red  inferior green  lateral yellow  medial orange  The medial and  lateral segments are removed and submitted as perpendicular sections  and the  remainder of the specimen is serially sectioned to reveal a tan white cut  surface with a focal area of hemorrhage  measuring REMOVED_DATE x 0 3cm and a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE  cm and a tan brown area of firmness  measuring 1 0cm x 0 5cm along the  superior posterior border  The specimen is entirely submitted for analysis   Micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Medial segment  perpendicular sections  2 frags per cassette  ESS   Micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Lateral segment  perpendicular sections  multi frags  ESS   Micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  