Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final  Type  Surgical Pathology  Pathology Report  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  SKIN  OTHER THAN CYST  TAG OR DEBRIDEMENT  Accessioned On  REMOVED_DATE  DORFMAN  DAVID  MICHAEL  M D  PH D   DIAGNOSIS  by DORFMAN DAVID MICHAEL M D  PH D   SKIN  LEFT BREAST   Skin showing a mild superficial interstitial lymphoid infiltrate  with minimal cytologic atypia  There are admixed histiocytes present   Very focal epidermotropism is identified  The dermis shows sclerosis  and mild vascular ectasia   Immunoperoxidase studies performed on formalin fixed paraffin sections  reveal the majority of cells are reactive for T cell markers  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Only occasional cells show  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  reactivity  There is a mixture  of  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  positive cells  Approximately 50  of the cells are  reactive for  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  stain highlights only a few cells   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  stain  is negative   NOTE   The findings raise the possibility of a T cell lymphoproliferative  disorder but are not diagnostic  Tissue will be submitted for gene  rearrangement studies and the results will be reported in an addendum   This case has been reviewed by Dr  Philip McKee  Dermatopathologist   SURGICAL ADDENDUM  by LINDEMAN NEAL I  M D   DNA was isolated from frozen skin  left breast  and analyzed by polymerase  chain reaction  using consensus primers to the V and J regions of the T Cell  receptor gamma chain gene   Results  A single  intense  discrete band was obtained with primers to Vgamma  region 11  identical in REMOVED_DATE aliquots  A reproducible pattern of multiple faint  bands was obtained with primers to region Vg9  and smears were obtained with  primers to regions Vg1 8  Vg10   These results are consistent with a monoclonal T cell population   CLINICAL DATA   History  58 year old white female with 3 month history of asymptomatic atrophic  telangiectatic plaques on medial breasts and proximal thighs   Operation  Excision  Operative findings  Repeat biopsy for tissue markers  Clinical Diagnosis  Rule out CTCL vs lymphoproliferative disorder   TISSUE SUBMITTED  Skin   left breast   GROSS DESCRIPTION  by O CONNELL FIONNUALA  The specimen is received in saline  labeled with the patient s name and unit  number only  and consists of a skin punch measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 3cm which is  bisected  One piece is fixed in formalin and one piece is snap frozen for  potential gene rearrangement studies and or immunoperoxidase studies   Micro 1  1 frag  ESS   A second specimen is received in formalin on REMOVED_DATE  labeled with the  patient s name and unit number only  and consists of a skin punch measuring  0 3 x REMOVED_DATE x 0 3cm   Micro 2  1 frag   ESS   Reports to  O SULLIVAN HUNNEWELL  REBECCA  SPECIMEN TYPE  SKIN  OTHER THAN CYST  TAG OR DEBRIDEMENT  ADDITIONAL SPECIMEN TYPE  SKIN  OTHER THAN CYST  TAG OR DEBRIDEMENT   report_end  , no_TubularCancer.
