Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final  Type  Surgical Pathology  Pathology Report  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OUTSIDE SLIDE CONSULT   BREAST  Accessioned On  REMOVED_DATE  LESTER  SUSAN  M D  PH D   DIAGNOSIS  by LESTER SUSAN M D  PH D   CONSULT SLIDES FROM HARVARD VANGUARD MEDICAL ASSOCIATES  BOSTON  MA   RIGHT BREAST  ULTRASOUND GUIDED CORE NEEDLE BIOPSIES FOR A REMOVED_DATE CM CIRCUMSCRIBED  MASS   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   10 2 02    Florid epithelial hyperplasia with atypia involving fragments of a  fibroadenoma   Note  The hyperplasia has areas of cribriforming and nuclear atypia   Although a diagnosis of atypical ductal hyperplasia cannot be made due to  the difficulty of evaluating such lesions within a fibroadenoma  the  atypia is sufficient enough to warrent evaluation after excision to  exclude a more diagnostic lesion in adjacent tissue   CLINICAL DATA   History  None given   TISSUE SUBMITTED  Consult slides   GROSS DESCRIPTION  by PREISINGER ELIZABETH ANN M D   Received from Harvard Vanguard Medical Center  Pathology Department  120  Brookline Avenue  Boston  MA  REMOVED_CASE_ID   are eight slides   The slides are labeled  S 02  REMOVED_CASE_ID   and sublabeled  A   four slides  and   B   four slides  corresponding to a right breast ultrasound guided core needle  biopsy performed on REMOVED_DATE  according to the accompanying pathology report  bearing the patient s name   Reports to  CARTER  KATHARINE M D   SPECIMEN TYPE  OUTSIDE SLIDE CONSULT   BREAST   report_end  , no_TubularCancer.
 REMOVED_CASE_ID  HAMWEY ANGELA 46 F  REG REF 12 14    REMOVED_CASE_ID   WIC AUGUST ALLISON M D                                                                                             Specimen   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_CASE_ID  SOUT Received  12 14 06  REMOVED_CASE_ID  Spec Type    Clinical diagnosis   history  Suspicious clustered microcalcifications of upper outer quadrant of right breast and solid nodules of upper inner quadrant near nipple   1  ductal carcinoma in situ vs fibrocystic changes   2  solid nodule    fibroadenoma Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT MURJ Gross description  The specimen is received in formalin in two containers  Specimen A labeled  guided breast biopsies  right breast  lateral  1  10 o clock  and consists of a 1 x 1 x 0 2cm  aggregate of tan white  firm  glistening needle biopsies admixed with blood clot measuring REMOVED_DATE to 0 2cm  in diameter  1 agg  all  Specimen B labeled  guided breast biopsies  right breast  12 30  and consists of four firm  yellowish white  glistening needle biopsies measuring 1 7cm  in length x 0 2cm  in diameter to 2 5cm  in length x 0 2cm  in diameter  4X all  Dictated by  LV Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT MURJ DIAGNOSIS  A  Right breast  10 o clock  needle core biopsy  Atypical ductal hyperplasia  see note   No microcalcifications identified  multiple level sections were obtained  B  Right breast  12 30  needle core biopsy  DIAGNOSIS   Continued  Fibroadenoma  Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT MURJ DESCRIPTIVE COMMENT  NOTE  Specimen A  needle core biopsy at 10 o clock  was sent to Dr  Frederick Koerner at Massachusetts General Hospital  He concurs with the diagnosis  His note and recommendation for excision reads as follows   The sections of this core biopsy specimen show a background of slight fibrocystic changes  Atypical epithelial cells occupy a small duct and associated lobules  Those in the duct have columnar shapes and they possess slightly enlarged nuclei and eosinophilic apical cytoplasm  I would classify these cells as atypical columnar cells and the proliferation as an especially mild form of atypical ductal hyperplasia  The cells in the nearby acini display more pronounced atypia  They look large and they contain large nuclei  pale  homogeneous chromatin  small nucleoli  and abundant  eosinophilic cytoplasm  The cells pile up slightly and  thereby  fill and slightly distend the glands  I regard these cells  too  as atypical ductal cells and the focus as atypical ductal hyperplasia  It could represent a small portion of a ductal carcinoma in situ  A few other acini distant from this region contain atypical epithelial cells that look a bit different  They appear slightly smaller  the nuclei look smaller and darker and the nucleoli seem less evident  moreover  the cytoplasm has a grey blue color  I cannot confidently identify the nature of these cells  but I suspect that they may represent small regions of lobular neoplasia  atypical lobular hyperplasia   In light of the presence of these atypical epithelial proliferations and the absence of calcifications  I would suggest that the patient and her physicians seriously consider an excision of this region of the right breast   Final Diagnosis by  Nicole A  Mihok  M D  Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT MURJ Notes  REMOVED_DATE  7 slides ret d from NEMC  jam   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_DATE  NEMC report rec d  djd REMOVED_DATE Per Dr  Homa Safaii  NEMC Path  sent 7  A s  to her  dg  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_DATE  7 slides ret d from MGH  djd CR REMOVED_DATE  Path report faxed from MGH  jam REMOVED_DATE  Report  letter  faxed from MGH  jam REMOVED_DATE  7 slides  all A  to Dr  Frederick Koerner  MGH Pathology for opinion per NAM djd CO Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT DORD REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT MURJ REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT MURJ Notes   Continued  REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  PAT DORD REM